.\" raz(1) - the raz compiler
.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt RAZ 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm raz
.Nd compile a raz program to a Linux executable
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar option ...
.Ar Main.raz
.Nm
.Cm run
.Op Ar option ...
.Ar Main.raz
.Op Ar arg ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
compiles the module in
.Ar Main.raz ,
and every module it imports, to an executable for x86-64 Linux, with
no assembler, linker or C library in between.
A program that names a C library in a
.Ic link
line, or declares an
.Ic extern
procedure, is linked against C dynamically; any other is static.
.Pp
A module
.Ar Name
is found as
.Ar Name Ns .linux-x86_64.raz
or
.Ar Name Ns .raz
in the directory of
.Ar Main.raz ,
then in each
.Fl I
directory in turn, then in the user library directories (see
.Sx ENVIRONMENT ) ,
then in the library,
.Pa lib/
beside the
.Nm
executable, whose modules
.Xr raz-lib 3
says.
A program has one module of each name: a module found in two of these
places is an error, unless one of the files is a symbolic link to the
other, which chooses it.
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm run
compiles the same way, then runs the program with the
.Ar arg Ns s ,
writing no file: its output and exit status are those of the command.
.Pp
Every array index and slice is checked, unless the module says
.Ic check off ;
a failed check, a nil pointer, a division by zero or a stack overflow
ends the program with status 2, and says on standard error where it
was, the calls under way, and their variables:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Bad.raz:6:7: index 5 is out of range for a length of 3
  in Bad.main at Bad.raz:6:7: i = 5
.Ed
.Pp
The executable has a symbol table and line numbers for
.Xr gdb 1 ,
.Xr nm 1
and
.Xr perf 1 ,
unless built with
.Fl s .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-noprofile"
.It Fl o Ar file
Write the executable to
.Ar file .
Left out, it is
.Ar Main.raz
without
.Pa .raz .
.Cm run
takes no
.Fl o .
.It Fl I Ar dir
Look for imported modules in
.Ar dir
too, after the main module's own directory and before the library.
It may be given up to 16 times.
An error in a module found in such a directory, or in one of
.Ev RAZ_PATH ,
names that directory in its path; and if the directory holds a file
.Pa RAZ-VERSION ,
of one line such as
.Ql 0.21 ,
a second line says the raz the library was written for and this one:
.Ql (a library written for raz 0.18; this is raz 0.21) .
The compiler only reads the file; it never refuses to build for it.
.It Fl Werror
A warning counts as an error.
.It Fl S
List the main module's machine code, each instruction under the line
of source it was made for, on standard output, and write no
executable.
.It Fl s
Strip the program of what is there for finding faults: its symbol
table, its line numbers, the places a failure is told by, and the
profiler.
A failure then says only its address, and the program is much
smaller: hello, world is 7 KB, against 28 KB.
.It Fl base Ar addr
Load the program at
.Ar addr ,
not 400000H, as a kernel may want: a multiple of 4096, written as raz
writes a number, as
.Cm 0FFFFFFFF80000000H .
The program must lie wholly in the low or the top 2 GB of the address
space, which a 32-bit address reaches.
.It Fl bare
Make a program for the bare machine, a kernel, with no Linux under it.
Its entry point reads no arguments, so
.Fn ARGC
is 0, and runs no body of
.Cm Rt ,
whose system calls set the handlers of signals: a nil pointer or a
division fault is not told.
A failed check is still told, by a system call the kernel must catch.
Its stack must be 16-byte aligned, as Linux leaves it, and its bss
zeroed.
A program that uses C is refused.
.It Fl noprofile
Leave out the profiler, which
.Ev RAZ_PROFILE
turns on, but keep the rest.
.It Fl libc Ar name
Link a program that uses C against
.Ar name ,
.Cm glibc
or
.Cm musl .
Left out, it is glibc, or musl on a machine that has musl's loader,
.Pa /lib/ld-musl-x86_64.so.1 ,
and not glibc's, as Alpine has.
A program without C needs no C library, and runs on either.
.It Fl h , Fl \-help
Say the options.
.It Fl \-version
Say the version of
.Nm ,
and the version of the report it follows.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "RAZ_PROFILE"
.It Ev RAZ_PATH
Colon-separated directories of installed raz source modules, searched
after the
.Fl I
directories and before the standard library.
When unset, the user library directory is
.Pa $HOME/.raz/lib ;
with no nonempty
.Ev HOME ,
there is no default.
An explicitly empty
.Ev RAZ_PATH
disables user library directories; it does not disable the standard
library. Empty entries are ignored, not taken as the working directory.
Relative paths are relative to the compiler's working directory.
The compiler does not expand a tilde or variables within paths.
Directories are not searched recursively: install a library's source
modules directly in one of them, with their target-specific siblings.
The duplicate-module error and symbolic-link exception apply across
all search directories;
.Fl I
does not override an installed module.
.It Ev RAZ_PROFILE
Set to anything, in the environment of a program built without
.Fl s
or
.Fl noprofile ,
it has the program sample itself every millisecond of its time, and
say at its end, on standard error, which procedures took the most of
it, each with its share in its own code and its share in all, with the
procedures it called, and which places in the source:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
profile: 521 samples of a millisecond; each procedure's own, then with its calls'
   16%     27%  Screen.put
    9%     93%  Parser.feed
by place:
    8%  src/Screen.raz:275:5
.Ed
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 if it wrote the program, and 1 if it found an error, which it
says on standard error, with the line of source and a mark under the
place.
.Nm
.Cm run
exits as the program does.
.Sh LISTING
The listing of
.Fl S
is in lines of three kinds, which a tool may read:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
a blank line, then a procedure's name as
.Ql Module.name: ;
.It
a source line: its number, right-aligned in 6 columns, two spaces, and
the line's text as it is in the file;
.It
an instruction: its offset in the procedure, right-aligned in 14
columns, two spaces, and the instruction as an assembler would read it;
a jump within the procedure gives its target as an offset in the same
way, and a call of a procedure names it.
.El
.Pp
The listing is of the main module's live procedures, those the program
uses, in the order their code was made.
Nothing is disassembled: each instruction is the one the compiler meant
to write.
A source line comes before the first instruction made for it, and is
not repeated until the line changes.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compile and run hello, world:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
raz Hello.raz && ./Hello
raz run Hello.raz
.Ed
.Pp
Build a small program for shipping, then see where its time goes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
raz -s -o hello Hello.raz
raz -o prog Prog.raz && RAZ_PROFILE=1 ./prog
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr raz-lib 3 ,
the library;
.Xr raz-tutorial 7 ,
a tour of the language;
.Xr raz 7 ,
the language at a glance;
.Xr gdb 1 .
The report,
.Pa doc/raz.md ,
defines the language, and the guide to the compiler,
.Pa doc/compiler.md ,
walks through its sources.
